M9-01-CONSIDERATION.2:
Structure the acquisition consideration for the friendly tender offer on **Target Spa**.

Offer data:
- **Number of Target Shares**: {p0}
- **Offer Price per Target Share**: {p1}
- **Current Acquirer Share Price**: {p2}
- **Consideration Structure**: {p3} Cash / {p4} Stock

The cash part must be funded (cash on the balance sheet or new debt); the stock part is paid by issuing new acquirer shares at its current price, and dilutes its shareholders. The **exchange ratio** says how many acquirer shares each target shareholder receives for each share.

Compute the offer value, cash required, new shares to issue and the exchange ratio for the stock part.

M9-03-PPAGOODWILL.2:
Carry out the **Purchase Price Allocation (PPA)** for the acquisition of the target.

Accounting parameters:
- **Equity Purchase Price**: {p0}
- **Book Equity (Target NBV)**: {p1}
- **Step-up on Amortisable Intangibles**: {p2}
- **Intangibles Useful Life**: {usefulLife} years
- **Tax Rate (IRES)**: {p4}

The price paid is allocated to the target's assets and liabilities at fair value. The revaluation of intangibles (step-up) has no tax value: the extra amortisation won't be deductible, so a **deferred tax liability** (DTL) arises. Whatever the revalued equity, net of the DTL, doesn't explain is **goodwill**.

Compute the DTL, the residual goodwill and how much the step-up amortisation will reduce the acquirer's net income each year.

M9-04-ACCRETIONDILUTION.2:
Assess the economic impact of the merger on the acquirer's pro-forma **EPS (Earnings per Share)**.

Standalone & Transaction data:
- **Acquirer Net Income**: {p0} on {p1} shares (EPS {p2})
- **Target Net Income**: {p3}
- **Post-Tax Net Synergies**: +{p4}
- **Post-Tax New Debt Financing Costs**: -{p5}
- **Post-Tax PPA Amortisation**: -{p6}
- **New Shares Issued for the Deal**: {p7}

The deal is **accretive** if the acquirer's EPS after the deal is higher than standalone, **dilutive** if it's lower. Combined earnings rise (target earnings and synergies), but fall because of the financing cost and the PPA amortisation; the denominator grows with the new shares issued.

Compute pro-forma earnings and EPS, and the accretion (positive) or dilution (negative) as a percentage.

M9-05-BREAKEVENSYNERGIES.2:
The proposed M&A deal is currently dilutive for the Acquirer's shareholders.
Determine the minimum amount of **Breakeven Pre-Tax Synergies** needed to reach accretion of exactly 0.0%.

Pro-forma data:
- **Acquirer Standalone EPS (Target to preserve)**: {p0}
- **Post-Merger Pro-Forma Share Count**: {p1}
- **Base Pro-Forma Net Income (Without Synergies)**: {p2}
- **Tax Rate (IRES)**: {p3}

Work backwards: what net income would be needed, with the pro-forma shares, to have the same EPS as today? The difference from base earnings is an **after-tax** number; the synergies management promises are instead **pre-tax**, and each euro of synergy brings home only its net part.

Compute the required earnings, the gap to close and the pre-tax breakeven synergies.

M9-06-EPSBRIDGE.8:
The board has seen that the deal changes EPS and wants to know **why**. Build the bridge from the acquirer's standalone EPS to pro-forma EPS, one item per row, all expressed in euros per pro-forma share.

- Acquirer net income {p0}, {p1} shares
- Target net income {p2}; after-tax synergies {p3}
- After-tax financing cost {p4}; after-tax PPA amortisation {p5}
- New shares issued {p6}

The first item is the most overlooked: even before counting any target earnings, issuing shares spreads existing earnings over more heads. Items that reduce EPS should be written as negatives, so the bridge adds up from top to bottom.

M9-07-PROFORMALEVERAGE.2:
Analyse the financial sustainability and leverage of the post-merger combined entity.

Balance sheet and transaction data:
- **Acquirer EBITDA**: {p0} | **Acquirer Net Debt**: {p1}
- **Target EBITDA**: {p2} | **Target Net Debt**: {p3}
- **Run-Rate Synergies**: {p4}
- **New Bank Debt Raised for the Deal**: {p5}
- **Maximum Leverage to Keep an Investment Grade Rating**: {p6}

After closing the group carries the debt of both companies plus what was raised to pay for the acquisition. The agencies look at combined leverage: above the threshold the rating falls and the cost of debt rises. How much room is left, in euros, before hitting it?

Compute combined EBITDA and net debt, pro-forma leverage and the additional debt still sustainable at the threshold (negative if the threshold is already exceeded).
